                "html": "<h4 class=\"center\">Goya's Artistic Duality</h4><p>Francisco Goya, in embracing the simplicity and truth in his art, masterfully navigated the delicate balance between idealism and reality. Among the many royal portraits of his career, his most significant [[23]] was from Charles IV, in which he abandoned conventional majesty to faithfully reveal the monarch, whose depiction was later likened to a [[24]]. His subsequent royal paintings, including the 1815 unflattering portrait of Ferdinand VII, show that he was able to depict truth without jeopardizing his position.</p><p>However, Goya's private portraiture revealed a different narrative, as exemplified by his 1820 double portrait with Dr. Arrieta, in which an ailing Goya is seen in the care of the good [[25]]. It serves as a testament to Goya's pinnacle ability to portray humanity in both its finest and darkest moments, all while refraining from passing judgment; in the latter scenario, individuals may have to be dependent on the [[26]] of their fellow men.</p>"
